  "answer": "To reduce the risk of unreliable classifications on unfamiliar images (those never encountered before) or maliciously altered images (adversarial attacks), it has been proposed to have the classifier indicate uncertainty instead of providing a classification. Alternatively, the system can act conservatively by requiring that multiple different kinds of classifiers agree on the result before outputting a decision, or by testing different classes of models to see which are robust (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hWb09uq6Zlk). In general, systems can be designed to simply withhold an output when uncertain (https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/dYHiMeSdLrrX3cy4a/embedding-safety-in-ml-development)."
